00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.920
So, what is machine learning?

00:00:02.920 --> 00:00:07.820
In this video, you learn the definition of what it is, and also get a sense of when you

00:00:07.820 --> 00:00:09.320
might want to apply it.

00:00:09.320 --> 00:00:11.480
Let's take a look together.

00:00:11.480 --> 00:00:17.340
Here's the definition of what is machine learning that is attributed to Arthur Samuel.

00:00:17.340 --> 00:00:22.160
He defined machine learning as the field of study that gives computers the ability to learn

00:00:22.160 --> 00:00:24.880
without being explicitly programmed.

00:00:24.880 --> 00:00:31.060
Another claim to fame was that back in the 1950s, he wrote the Checkers Playing Program.

00:00:31.060 --> 00:00:38.120
The amazing thing about this program was that Arthur Samuel himself wasn't a very good checkers player.

00:00:38.120 --> 00:00:43.160
What he did was he had programmed the computer to play maybe tens of thousands of games against

00:00:43.160 --> 00:00:44.360
itself.

00:00:44.360 --> 00:00:49.060
By watching what sorts of board positions tended to lead to wins and what positions tended

00:00:49.060 --> 00:00:54.680
to lead to losses, the Checkers Playing Program learned over time what a good or bad board

00:00:54.680 --> 00:00:56.080
positions.

00:00:56.080 --> 00:01:01.940
By trying to get to good and avoid bad positions, his program learned to get better and better

00:01:01.940 --> 00:01:04.040
at playing checkers.

00:01:04.040 --> 00:01:08.000
Because the computer had the patience to play tens of thousands of games against itself,

00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:13.840
it was able to get so much checkers playing experience that eventually it became a better

00:01:13.840 --> 00:01:17.720
checkers player than Arthur Samuel himself.

00:01:17.720 --> 00:01:22.300
Now throughout these videos, besides me trying to talk about stuff, I'll occasionally

00:01:22.300 --> 00:01:26.740
ask you a question to help make sure you understand the content.

00:01:26.740 --> 00:01:31.100
Here's one about what happens if the computer had played far fewer games.

00:01:31.100 --> 00:01:38.950
Please take a look and pick whichever you think is a better answer.

00:01:38.950 --> 00:01:40.950
Thanks for looking at the quiz.

00:01:40.950 --> 00:01:49.030
And so if you had selected this answer, would it made it worse, then you got it right.

00:01:49.030 --> 00:01:53.670
In general, the more opportunities you give a learning algorithm to learn, the better it

00:01:53.670 --> 00:01:55.110
will perform.

00:01:55.110 --> 00:01:59.590
If you didn't select the correct answer the first time, that's totally okay too.

00:01:59.590 --> 00:02:03.550
The point of these quiz questions isn't to see if you can get them all correct on the

00:02:03.550 --> 00:02:04.930
first try.

00:02:04.930 --> 00:02:09.630
These questions are here just to help you practice the concepts you're learning.

00:02:09.630 --> 00:02:14.510
Arthur Samuel's definition was a rather informal one, but in the next two videos, we'll dive

00:02:14.510 --> 00:02:21.110
deeper together into what are the major types of machine learning algorithms.

00:02:21.110 --> 00:02:25.070
In this class, you learn about many different learning algorithms.

00:02:25.070 --> 00:02:31.190
The two main types of machine learning are supervised learning and unsupervised learning.

00:02:31.190 --> 00:02:36.710
We'll define what these terms mean more in the next couple videos.

00:02:36.710 --> 00:02:42.410
Of these two, supervised learning is the type of machine learning that is used most in many

00:02:42.410 --> 00:02:49.250
real world applications, and that has seen the most rapid advancement and innovation.

00:02:49.330 --> 00:02:54.930
In this specialization, which has three causes in total, the first and second causes will

00:02:54.930 --> 00:03:00.330
focus on supervised learning, and the third will focus on unsupervised learning, recommended

00:03:00.330 --> 00:03:03.530
systems and reinforcement learning.

00:03:03.530 --> 00:03:09.370
By far, the most used types of learning algorithms today are supervised learning, unsupervised

00:03:09.370 --> 00:03:12.450
learning, and recommended systems.

00:03:12.450 --> 00:03:16.610
The other thing we're going to spend a lot of time on in this specialization is

00:03:16.610 --> 00:03:20.610
practical advice for applying learning algorithms.

00:03:20.610 --> 00:03:23.250
This is something I feel pretty strongly about.

00:03:23.250 --> 00:03:29.090
Teaching about learning algorithms is like giving someone a set of tools, and equally important

00:03:29.090 --> 00:03:34.610
or even more important than making sure you have great tools is making sure you know

00:03:34.610 --> 00:03:36.490
how to apply them.

00:03:36.490 --> 00:03:41.650
Because you know, what good is it if someone were to give you a state of the art hammer

00:03:41.650 --> 00:03:44.210
or a state of the art hand drill and say, good luck.

00:03:44.210 --> 00:03:48.290
Now you have all the tools you need to build a three story house doesn't really work like

00:03:48.290 --> 00:03:49.610
that.

00:03:49.610 --> 00:03:55.210
And so too in machine learning, making sure you have the tools is really important.

00:03:55.210 --> 00:04:00.970
And so it's making sure that you know how to apply the tools of machine learning effectively.

00:04:00.970 --> 00:04:05.170
So that's what you get in this class, the tools as well as the skills that apply them

00:04:05.170 --> 00:04:07.050
effectively.

00:04:07.050 --> 00:04:11.990
I regularly visit with friends and teams in some of the top tech companies.

00:04:11.990 --> 00:04:17.510
And even today, I see experienced machine learning teams apply machine learning algorithms

00:04:17.510 --> 00:04:19.110
to some problems.

00:04:19.110 --> 00:04:23.850
And sometimes they've been going at it for six months without much success.

00:04:23.850 --> 00:04:27.550
And when I look at what they're doing, I sometimes feel like I could have told them

00:04:27.550 --> 00:04:30.550
six months ago that the current approach won't work.

00:04:30.550 --> 00:04:34.430
And there's a different way of using these tools that will give them a much better chance

00:04:34.430 --> 00:04:35.990
of success.

00:04:35.990 --> 00:04:40.750
So in this class, one of the relatively unique things you learn is you learn a lot

00:04:40.750 --> 00:04:46.230
about the best practices for how to actually develop a practical, valuable machine learning

00:04:46.230 --> 00:04:47.510
system.

00:04:47.510 --> 00:04:52.030
This way, you're less likely to end up in one of those teams that end up losing six months

00:04:52.030 --> 00:04:54.590
going in the wrong direction.

00:04:54.590 --> 00:04:59.230
In this class, you gain a sense of how the most skilled machine learning engineers build

00:04:59.230 --> 00:05:00.230
systems.

00:05:00.230 --> 00:05:05.270
And I hope you finish this class as one of those very rare people in today's world

00:05:05.270 --> 00:05:09.810
that know how to design and build serious machine learning systems.

00:05:09.810 --> 00:05:11.970
So that's machine learning.

00:05:11.970 --> 00:05:17.750
In the next video, let's look more deeply at what is supervised learning, and also what

00:05:17.750 --> 00:05:19.970
is unsupervised learning.

00:05:19.970 --> 00:05:25.090
In addition, you learn when you might want to use each of them, supervised and unsupervised

00:05:25.090 --> 00:05:26.090
learning.

00:05:26.090 --> 00:05:27.530
I'll see you in the next video.
